    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Founded by the former herdsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire-dombo">Changamire Dombo I</a>, the Rozvi Empire dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a> from the late 17th century.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire-dombo">Dombo</a> drove Portuguese traders and settlers from the interior in the 1690s, keeping the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade under African control.</li><li>Drought, a shift in coastal demand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nguni">Nguni</a> invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mfecane">Mfecane</a> destroyed the state in the 1830s; its stone ruins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mwari-cult">Mwari cult</a> endured.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mwari-cult">Mwari cult</a> patronised by the Rozvi outlived the empire and helped organise resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> rule in 1896-1897.</p></aside>
    </section>

