    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#changamire-dombo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/changamire-dombo.jpg" alt="Changamire Dombo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#changamire-dombo">Changamire Dombo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire-rozvi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ypirétis, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rozvi Empire emerged in the late seventeenth century on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a>, born of a decisive military and political rupture within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> state. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire-dombo">Changamire Dombo I</a>, began his career as a herdsman and subordinate under the Mutapa ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mukombwe">Mukombwe</a>, who entrusted him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> and regional authority in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guruuswa">Guruuswa</a> region from around the 1670s. Using cattle-based patronage to build personal loyalty, Dombo assembled a disciplined force of perhaps 2,000 to 3,000 warriors that answered to him rather than to the Mutapa's tributary levies. In 1684, following an open breach with his former overlord, Dombo defeated a combined force of Mutapa troops and their Portuguese allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-maungwe">Battle of Maungwe</a>, a victory that both asserted independence from an African suzerain and repelled European colonial forces, an achievement with few parallels among seventeenth-century southern African polities. He then overran the older Torwa state of Butua in the south-west and, in campaigns of 1693–1695, expelled Portuguese traders, missionaries and <em>prazo</em>-holders (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prazero">prazeros</a>) from the plateau interior, driving them back towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zambezi">Zambezi</a> valley and the Mozambican lowlands. The Rozvi inherited the stone-building tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> (c. 1100–1450 CE) and its Torwa successor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khami">Khami</a>, occupying and building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> enclosures at sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#danangombe">Danangombe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naletale">Naletale</a> that served as administrative, ceremonial and residential centres for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire">Changamire</a>'s court. The enabling conditions for the Rozvi's rise were the Mutapa state's progressive weakening under Portuguese commercial penetration, the precedent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-zimbabwe-culture">Zimbabwe-culture</a> political organisation, and Dombo's rare combination of military talent and cattle-based patronage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#zambezi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zambezi.jpg" alt="Zambezi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#zambezi">Zambezi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zambezi_River_at_junction_of_Namibia,_Zambia,_Zimbabwe_%26_Botswana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brian McMorrow, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rozvi economy rested on three interlocking systems: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>-based patronage, the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade, and agricultural tribute from a predominantly Shona-speaking population across the plateau. Cattle functioned not merely as livestock but as the primary medium of political exchange: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire">Changamire</a> distributed herds to loyal chiefs and regional administrators in return for tribute, military service and allegiance, binding peripheral elites to the centre through material dependency rather than administrative coercion. Gold mining had sustained the plateau's economies since at least the eleventh century CE, and the Rozvi controlled access to the gold-producing regions, channelling exports through trading fairs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zumbo">Zumbo</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zambezi">Zambezi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#masekesa">Masekesa</a> in Manica, where Portuguese and Afro-Portuguese merchants exchanged cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bead">beads</a> and manufactured goods for gold and ivory under the Changamire's oversight. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kuruva">kuruva</a> system, traders operating within Rozvi territory were required to pay tribute to the Changamire's court, generating steady revenue without complex bureaucratic machinery. Agriculture centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sorghum">sorghum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a>, supplemented over time by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maize">maize</a> introduced through Portuguese coastal networks. Population figures are necessarily approximate, but a territory of perhaps 250,000 square kilometres likely supported several hundred thousand people organised into hierarchical chieftaincies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mwari-cult">Mwari</a> religious cult, centred on oracular shrines in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matopos-hills">Matopos Hills</a>, lent ideological legitimacy to the Changamire and provided a further channel of redistribution through ritual gift exchange between the court and the cult's priesthood.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mfecane"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mfecane.jpg" alt="Mfecane" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mfecane">Mfecane</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Departure_of_the_Fingoes-1840.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rozvi military system marked a significant evolution from the armed forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> state, combining professional discipline with strategic mobility across the open <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#savannah">savannah</a> and granite-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kopje">kopje</a> terrain of the plateau. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire-dombo">Changamire Dombo I</a>'s core innovation was a standing body of trained warriors maintained through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> patronage rather than seasonal levy, enabling rapid response and sustained campaigning that tributary militias could not provide. Tactically, the Rozvi relied chiefly on infantry armed with spears, clubs and shields, favouring surprise, encirclement and shock in broken ground, a mode of warfare that rendered Portuguese musketry less decisive than European commanders expected. The most dramatic demonstration of Rozvi capability came in 1693, when Dombo, answering an appeal from the Mutapa claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nyakunembire">Nyakunembire</a>, destroyed the Portuguese settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dambarare">Dambarare</a> and swept away the Portuguese interior fairs in a remarkably short campaign; the fair at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#masekesa">Masekesa</a> in Manica fell in 1695, shortly before Dombo's death. Subsequent operations maintained this defensive perimeter against periodic Portuguese attempts to re-establish inland footholds in the early eighteenth century. For much of that century the army operated largely in a deterrent capacity, enforcing tribute from subordinate chiefdoms. The Rozvi's fundamental military vulnerability, exposed only in the 1820s and 1830s, lay in their unfamiliarity with the regimental organisation and close-combat stabbing-spear tactics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nguni">Nguni</a> forces displaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mfecane">Mfecane</a>, against which the loosely organised Rozvi levies proved unable to stand.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#matopos-hills"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matopos-hills.jpg" alt="Matopos Hills" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#matopos-hills">Matopos Hills</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunrise_Matobo_Zimbabwe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Macvivo at English Wikipedia (Original text: Samwise Gamgee), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rozvi Empire was governed through a hierarchy centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire">Changamire</a>, a title designating both the ruler and the dynasty, whose authority combined political, military and religious dimensions in a manner characteristic of plateau statecraft. Regional government was delegated to subordinate chiefs drawn both from the Rozvi ruling lineage and from incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shona">Shona</a> dynasties, whose loyalty was maintained through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> patronage and reinforced by the Changamire's symbolic connection to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mwari-cult">Mwari cult</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matopos-hills">Matopos</a> shrines. Unlike the looser tributary confederacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a>, the Rozvi state exercised tighter direct control over its core territory, with stone enclosures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#danangombe">Danangombe</a> functioning as physical embodiments of central authority: administrative headquarters, royal residences and ceremonial centres at once. Justice and dispute resolution appear to have followed customary Shona legal traditions mediated by chiefs and royal officials, with serious cases referred upwards to the Changamire's court. Lacking a literate bureaucratic class of the kind found in contemporary Islamic or East Asian states, the empire relied on oral record-keeping, royal messengers and the personal authority of appointed officials. Succession followed patrilineal principles within the royal lineage, but the absence of strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#primogeniture">primogeniture</a> produced recurring disputes, particularly after the deaths of long-reigning rulers. The Mwari priesthood played a quasi-constitutional role, providing ritual sanction for the Changamire's rule and a channel of communication between the court and the plateau's diverse chiefly communities that reinforced the cattle-patronage political economy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#great-zimbabwe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Great Zimbabwe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_(33736918448).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Moore from Johannesburg, South Africa, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rozvi Empire's most significant immediate impact was the reversal of Portuguese commercial colonialism on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire-dombo">Dombo</a>'s campaigns of the 1690s expelled the European interior settlements and preserved the plateau's political and economic autonomy for well over a century beyond what the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a>'s trajectory had suggested was possible. Thereafter trade continued, but on Rozvi terms: Portuguese and Afro-Portuguese merchants were confined to licensed fairs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zumbo">Zumbo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#masekesa">Masekesa</a> on the margins of Rozvi territory, and the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade passed through Sena and Tete to the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quelimane">Quelimane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sofala">Sofala</a> under African rather than European control of the interior. This arrangement preserved African commercial agency within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> trading system at a time when European penetration was transforming coastal economies elsewhere on the continent. Culturally, the Rozvi sustained and extended the stone-building tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>; the decorated walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naletale">Naletale</a> and the terraced platforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#danangombe">Danangombe</a> are among the finest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> architecture produced on the plateau after the abandonment of Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> around 1450 CE, the mature expression of a distinctively Zimbabwean aesthetic and engineering tradition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mwari-cult">Mwari cult</a> that the Rozvi patronised proved remarkably durable, surviving the empire's political collapse and playing a significant role in organising resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-chimurenga">First Chimurenga</a> of 1896–1897. Rozvi political precedents also informed successor arrangements on the plateau, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> kingdom established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a> from around 1840.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mzilikazi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mzilikazi.jpg" alt="Mzilikazi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mzilikazi~detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rozvi Empire's collapse in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries resulted from the near-simultaneous failure of the systems that had sustained it, a compound crisis of unusual completeness. The first destabilising factor was environmental: severe droughts around the turn of the nineteenth century and again in the 1820s struck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>-based patronage economy at its foundation, depleting the herds that served as the primary medium of political exchange and disrupting the agricultural surplus on which tribute depended. The second was commercial: from the late eighteenth century Portuguese demand along the Mozambican coast shifted increasingly from gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> towards enslaved people, eroding the market for the plateau's principal exports and the revenues that had sustained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#changamire">Changamire</a>'s court and its redistributive capacity. The third and most violent factor was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mfecane">Mfecane</a>, the cascade of military displacement associated with the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> kingdom under Shaka from the 1810s, which sent successive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nguni">Nguni-speaking</a> groups across the plateau, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ngoni">Ngoni</a> in the early 1830s and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#swati">Swati</a> followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nyamazana-dlamini">Queen Nyamazana Dlamini</a>, whose forces killed the reigning Changamire, traditionally named as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chirisamhuru-ii">Chirisamhuru II</a>, in the mid-1830s. Internal succession disputes after the long eighteenth-century reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chirisamuru">Chirisamuru</a> had already weakened the institutional continuity that had held the state together, leaving later rulers ill-equipped to manage the pressures that followed. The arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> around 1840 completed the occupation of the former Rozvi heartland.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Rozvi Empire stands as one of the most significant achievements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-zimbabwe-culture">Zimbabwe-culture</a> tradition of stone-building statecraft, and its expulsion of the Portuguese from the interior plateau represents a rare and underappreciated assertion of African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">sovereignty</a> against European commercial imperialism in the seventeenth century.</em></p>

